{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\n`batch_dispatch.reroute_migrated_plans` (`src/rebar/reconciler/batch_dispatch.py:688-745` on origin/main dcb35ce77fe) selects a plan as migrated when every mutation's action is in `NON_CREATE_FAMILIES` and routes to the coordinator. It never checks direction. Its sibling `_ordered_create_plans` (`:395-406`) does check `direction == \"outbound\"`.\n\n`run_differs` plans every typed mutation, inbound and outbound (`run_differs.py:299-313`), and `apply_phase` passes those plans to `applier.apply`. So an inbound `update`, `conflict` or `repair_property` plan qualifies, and `run_migrated_reroute` dispatches it through `make_coordinator_dispatch` → `apply_handlers.dispatch_mutation`, the outbound handlers.\n\nReproduction: fake transport, real `applier.apply`, plans from `ticket_planner.plan_pass`. The probe is `inbound_probe/apply_e2e.py` in the `lane-ebf5` scratch directory.\n- With 1 inbound update for DIG-1 (payload `fields` plus 1 Jira comment) and 1 outbound update for DIG-2, the transport receives `update_issue('DIG-1')`, `add_comment('DIG-1', 'jira comment')` and `update_issue('DIG-2')`. The Jira-side change is written back to Jira, and the Jira comment is posted to Jira again.\n- With the inbound update alone, the transport receives no calls, because `_apply_batch` is not reached when there are no outbound dicts.\n- Same result at f5f666702b4. The selection filter has been unchanged since d49466b5e96 (2026-08-25, \"Wire live coordinator+fuse non-create reroute\").\n\nUnverified: a live pass. The fake transport accepts every call, so whether the real outbound comment path deduplicates against existing Jira comments has not been checked.\n\n## Reproduction (2026-10-24, current main)\nBuilt plans exactly as `run_differs` does (`plan_pass` over every typed mutation, inbound and outbound) and called `applier.apply` with a fake transport:\n- inbound update DIG-1 plus outbound update DIG-2: `update_issue` was called for DIG-1 as well as DIG-2.\n- inbound update carrying a Jira comment: `add_comment('DIG-1', ...)` posted that comment back to Jira.\nExisting applier tests never hit this because their `_plans` helper plans only the outbound mutations, which production does not.\n\n## Root cause\n`reroute_migrated_plans` (`src/rebar/reconciler/batch_dispatch.py`) selects plans by action family only. `apply()` applies inbound mutations locally through `_apply_typed` and sends only outbound dicts down the batch path, but the batch path also receives every ticket plan, and the coordinator dispatches each plan mutation through the outbound handlers. Its sibling `_ordered_create_plans` already requires `direction == \"outbound\"`. Plans group by target, so one plan can carry both directions.\n\n## Approach\nThis supersedes the earlier proposal to select only all-outbound plans: that would strand the outbound half of a mixed plan as an unrouted failure, so the outbound half is routed instead.\nIn `reroute_migrated_plans`, route only each plan's outbound mutations: a plan with none is skipped, and a mixed plan is trimmed to its outbound half with `dataclasses.replace`. The coordinator reads only a plan's identity, mutations and dependencies, so the trim is safe. The existing rule that a plan mixing create and non-create stays unrouted is unchanged.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope: `src/rebar/reconciler/batch_dispatch.py` and a new test module under `tests/unit/reconciler/apply/`. Out of scope: `reroute_ordered_create_plans`, the planner's grouping, and the inbound applier.\n\n## Testing\n```\npytest tests/unit/reconciler/apply/test_applier_inbound_plans_not_sent_to_jira.py -q\npytest tests/unit/reconciler -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 -q\nmake verify\n```\nCases: inbound update beside an outbound update on another issue; inbound update with a Jira comment; a mixed-direction plan on one issue; an inbound-only batch. Failure case: the first two fail on main because the transport receives the inbound writes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An inbound update plan never causes `update_issue` or `add_comment` on the transport (checked: new end-to-end tests, which fail on main).\n- [ ] A plan mixing directions on one issue sends only its outbound mutation (checked: new test).\n- [ ] The existing reconciler unit suite is unchanged apart from the 17 known pandoc failures on arm64 hosts (checked: pytest tests/unit/reconciler).\n- [ ] `make verify` is green on the Linux CI gate (`Verified +1` on the Gerrit change).\n\n## Follow-up\nWhether earlier bridge runs already wrote inbound changes back to Jira is a production check, tracked in its own ticket rather than held open here.\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1792827810848154001,"uuid":"8d6e7fc6-0c18-41e8-8ec9-99e660c2210b"}